<section class="chapter" aria-label="What signals are">
  <div class="chapter__inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">What signals are</h2>

    <p class="muted">
      Signals are the observable evidence behind how work actually happens — turning everyday activity into measurable inputs the system can use.
    </p>

    <p>
    Every hiring process generates thousands of small cues about how work moves, how people respond, and how decisions are made. Individually they look like noise. Collectively, they reveal how the system actually operates.
    </p>

    <p>
    Modern technology makes it possible to capture these patterns consistently and turn them into signals the system can measure. 
    </p>

    <p>
    Signals are not dashboards and they are not opinions. They are structured evidence the system can observe, compare, and act on.
    </p>


    <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4">
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Comparable</b>
        <span>Signals must mean the same thing across teams and tools.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Observable</b>
        <span>Signals are derived from real events or structured inputs.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Traceable</b>
        <span>Every signal can be tied to a workflow event or input.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Actionable</b>
        <span>Signals exist to support decisions, not reporting vanity.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      Without shared signals, organizations end up arguing about numbers instead of fixing the system.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

