<section class="chapter" aria-label="Signal types in depth">
  <div class="chapter__inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">Signal types in depth</h2>

    <details class="sys-details">
      <summary>
        <div class="sys-sum">
          <h3 class="section-title">Workflow Signals</h3>
          <p>How work moves through the hiring process and where the system slows down.</p>
          <span class="small">[ Expand section ]</span>
        </div>
        <span class="sys-chev">›</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="sys-body">
      
      <p class="muted">
      Workflow signals capture how the hiring process operates at a system level. They surface patterns in movement, timing, and coordination that often remain invisible until they become bottlenecks.
      </p>
      
      <p>
      Many signals commonly used in recruiting analytics describe the performance of the workflow itself rather than the performance of candidates. Pass-through rates, cycle times, and scheduling delays reveal how the process operates — not whether candidates are strong or weak.
      </p>
      
      <p>
      Because these signals describe the movement of work, they make operational friction visible. Patterns such as stage bottlenecks, coordination delays, and capacity constraints reveal where the hiring system is slowing down.
      </p>
      
      <p>
      Workflow signals form the operational foundation of the hiring system. They show whether the process itself is functioning efficiently before deeper behavioral or decision signals are considered.
      </p>

        <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4">
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Throughput</b>
            <span>Will pipeline volume get us to our hiring goals on time?</span>
          </div>

          <div class="mini">
            <b>Pass-through rates</b>
            <span>Where in the funnel are candidates being filtered out?</span>
          </div>

          <div class="mini">
            <b>Cycle time</b>
            <span>How quickly does the system move candidates from one decision to the next?</span>
          </div>

          <div class="mini">
            <b>Scheduling delays</b>
            <span>Where is coordination friction slowing the process down?</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4">
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Bottlenecks</b>
            <span>Where is work accumulating faster than it can move forward?</span>
          </div>

          <div class="mini">
            <b>Capacity constraints</b>
            <span>What structural limits are preventing the system from moving faster?</span>
          </div>
          
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Offer Acceptance</b>
            <span>Are delays in the hiring process causing us to lose candidates?</span>
          </div>
          
          <div class="mini">
          <b>Sourcing volume</b>
          <span>Are we bringing enough candidates into the pipeline?</span>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>

        <div class="callout">
          Workflow signals reveal how the process is functioning — not how people feel about it.
        </div>

        <h3 class="section-title--sub">Connected tools</h3>

        <div class="grid3">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Pipeline Health</h3>
            <p class="muted">Monitors live pipeline performance across stages to surface slowdowns and bottlenecks.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="card">
            <h3>Funnel Calculator</h3>
            <p class="muted">Models expected throughput and pass-through patterns against actual hiring volume.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="card">
            <h3>Workflow Diagnostics</h3>
            <p class="muted">Surfaces scheduling, coordination, and cycle-time anomalies inside the hiring process.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="sys-details">
      <summary>
        <div class="sys-sum">
          <h3 class="section-title">Behavioral Signals</h3>
          <p>Reveal how people perform inside the workflow — turning subjective patterns into measurable evidence.</p>
          <span class="small">[ Expand section ]</span>
        </div>
        <span class="sys-chev">›</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="sys-body">
        <p class="muted">
          Behavioral signals capture how people actually perform inside the hiring process.
          They surface patterns that are usually treated as subjective impressions and turn them into observable evidence the system can measure.
          </p>
          
          <p>
          Every hiring process generates behavioral signals: how candidates respond to communication, how they prepare, how they follow instructions, and how they incorporate feedback. These behaviors are visible during the process itself but are rarely captured consistently.
          </p>
          
          <p>
          When measured systematically, these patterns reveal how people perform inside the workflow rather than relying solely on interview impressions or retrospective evaluation.
          </p>

        <div class="grid2 grid2--equal">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Traditional Behavioral Interviews</h3>
            <p class="muted">
            Most hiring systems attempt to infer behavior through interview answers and interviewer interpretation.
            </p>
            <ul>
            <li>Candidates describe past experiences</li>
            <li>Interviewers interpret stories and examples</li>
            <li>Ratings are assigned using rubrics</li>
            <li>Decisions rely on subjective impressions</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
            Evidence is indirect and dependent on interpretation.
            </p>
            
          </div>

          <div class="card">
            <h3>Behavioral Signal Measurement</h3>
            <p class="muted">The candidate signal model identifies behaviors that appear naturally during the hiring process and captures them as structured signals.</p>
            <ul>
            <li>Responsiveness to communication</li>
            <li>Clarity of written communication</li>
            <li>Preparation for interviews</li>
            <li>Following instructions</li>
            <li>Attention to detail</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
            Evidence comes directly from real observations and interactions.
            </p>

          </div>
        </div>

        <h4>Candidate Signal Measurement</h4>
        <h4>How Behavioral Signals Are Used</h4>
        
        <p class="muted">
        Behavioral signals are not individual judgments. They are comparable inputs that allow the system to detect patterns across candidates, interviews, and hiring workflows.
        </p>
        <p>
        When captured consistently, behavioral signals make it possible to identify patterns that would otherwise remain anecdotal. Signals reveal where candidates demonstrate strong execution, communication, problem solving, or adaptability throughout the hiring process.
        </p>
        
        <p>
        These signals do not replace structured interviews. Instead, they provide additional evidence that complements interview feedback and supports more consistent decision making.
        </p>
        
        <div class="callout">
        Behavioral signals allow organizations to measure how candidates behave during the hiring process — not just how they describe past behavior.
        </div>

        <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4">
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Execution</b>
            <span>Signals that reveal follow-through, reliability, and how consistently tasks are completed.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="mini">
            <b>Communication</b>
            <span>Signals that reveal responsiveness, handoff quality, and how effectively someone works inside the process.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="mini">
            <b>Problem Solving</b>
            <span>Signals about how candidates analyze unfamiliar problems or structure solutions.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="mini">
            <b>Adaptability</b>
            <span>Signals that reveal how people respond to new information, feedback, or changing expectations.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ctaRow ctaRow--left">
          <a class="btn primary" href="/articles/candidate-behavioral-signals/">Read: Candidate Behavioral Signals in Hiring →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="sys-details">
      <summary>
        <div class="sys-sum">
          <h3 class="section-title">Decision Signals</h3>
          <p>Evaluate the quality of the decision process itself — not just outcomes.</p>
          <span class="small">[ Expand section ]</span>
        </div>
        <span class="sys-chev">›</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="sys-body">
        <p class="muted">
          Decision signals evaluate whether hiring decisions are consistent, calibrated, and defensible.
          They reveal how reliably the system is making decisions — not just what was decided.
        </p>

        <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4">
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Interviewer variance</b>
            <span>Divergence in ratings or recommendations across interviewers reviewing similar candidates.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="mini">
            <b>Rubric adherence</b>
            <span>How consistently structured evaluation criteria are applied across reviews.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="mini">
            <b>Override frequency</b>
            <span>How often recommendations are reversed outside the structured process.</span>
          </div>

          <div class="mini">
            <b>Calibration drift</b>
            <span>Gradual shifts in evaluation standards across a hiring team over time.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4">
          <div class="mini">
            <b>Decision latency</b>
            <span>Time elapsed between signal and decision, and what that delay reveals about process quality.</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          Decision signals evaluate the reliability of the decision process itself — not just the outcome it produced.
        </div>

        <div class="ctaRow ctaRow--left">
          <a class="btn primary" href="/system/logic/">Explore Decision Logic →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" aria-label="Why signals matter">
  <div class="chapter__inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">Why signals matter</h2>

    <p class="muted">
      Most organizations attempt to improve outcomes without measuring the inputs that produce them.
      Signals make operational patterns visible so root causes can be identified and addressed.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Reveal bottlenecks that slow hiring.</li>
      <li>Detect evaluation drift across recruiters and hiring managers.</li>
      <li>Measure behavioral factors that normally stay subjective.</li>
      <li>Provide consistent inputs for automation and decision support.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      Signals turn operational intuition into measurable patterns — enabling faster diagnosis and more consistent decisions.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

